#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308982 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0db14c2a-b6c3-5550-8ff6-c1a9e2b4ea5d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Quadratic Equation Given an Imaginary Root and Its Leading Coefficient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "da29b910-b7de-507f-9279-daa655082755" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{x}^{2}-2=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308984} {:answer/id #uuid "56e88c15-91d7-565b-82f3-bf726ee486b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{x}^{2}+3=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308985} {:answer/id #uuid "88aca4c2-1d2f-5228-a2da-cffb31a74262" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{x}^{2}+2=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308986} {:answer/id #uuid "2d2502c8-c042-513b-bbc6-0880c2ac691e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{x}^{2}-3=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308987} {:answer/id #uuid "3de3fdcb-71f5-5254-9433-21250163d792" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{x}^{2}+5=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308988}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3de3fdcb-71f5-5254-9433-21250163d792" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{x}^{2}+5=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308988} :answer-field/id #uuid "e5749261-77b3-5477-939e-e5a283bd4e18" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308983}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3c916945-8947-5d19-8097-13f17481334a" :question/math-academy-id "q-160006" :question/problem "A quadratic equation in the variable $x$ with real coefficients has a root $x=-\\sqrt{\\frac{5}{4}}\\text{ }\\,\\text{i}.$ Given that the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ in the equation is $4$, find the equation." :question/worked-solution "Since the quadratic equation has real coefficients and  $x=-\\sqrt{\\frac{5}{4}}\\text{ }\\,\\text{i}$ is a root, its conjugate  $x=\\sqrt{\\frac{5}{4}}\\text{ }\\,\\text{i}$ must also be a root. Therefore, using the zero product property, we can write a quadratic equation with the required roots as follows:  \n\n$$\n(x-\\sqrt{\\frac{5}{4}}\\text{ }\\,\\text{i})(x+\\sqrt{\\frac{5}{4}}\\text{ }\\,\\text{i})=0\n$$\n\n   \n\nMultiplying out the parentheses and simplifying gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-{(\\sqrt{\\frac{5}{4}}\\text{ }\\,\\text{i})}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-\\frac{5}{4}(-1) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+\\frac{5}{4} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation has roots $x=\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{5}{4}}\\text{ }\\,\\text{i},$ but the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ is not $4.$ Therefore, to find the desired equation, we multiply the equation above by $4.$ This gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4({x}^{2}+\\frac{5}{4}) & =0 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}+5 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308989 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0236acfd-59d1-5bc8-9e60-1c76ddc536f5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Quadratic Equation Given a Complex Root and Its Leading Coefficient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "94a379ff-62c4-5e0a-be29-d4f2d9a4a57e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{x}^{2}-16x-14=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308991} {:answer/id #uuid "354002cc-4fdd-5590-81a6-5e7ae7f09cab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{x}^{2}-4x+7=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308992} {:answer/id #uuid "8a560d02-4cb7-5347-8a4e-e782d189d0f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{x}^{2}+16x+14=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308993} {:answer/id #uuid "b3d5661d-5987-58ce-985c-e6087fa2f618" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{x}^{2}+8x+7=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308994} {:answer/id #uuid "40a6a17b-da1d-5c46-b206-52d26c15428b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{x}^{2}-8x+14=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308995}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "40a6a17b-da1d-5c46-b206-52d26c15428b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{x}^{2}-8x+14=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308995} :answer-field/id #uuid "22048be0-e859-5cfc-9c60-8936d0726416" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308990}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2095c4b9-c0bc-5ea3-a485-0afbc68ba43f" :question/math-academy-id "q-160008" :question/problem "A quadratic equation in the variable $x$ with real coefficients has a root $x=2+\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}.$ Given that the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ in the equation is $2$, find the equation." :question/worked-solution "Since the quadratic equation has real coefficients and $x=2+\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}$ is a root, its conjugate $x=2-\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}$ must also be a root. Therefore, using the zero product property, we can write a quadratic equation with the required roots as follows:\n\n$$\n(x-(2+\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}))(x-(2-\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}))=0\n$$\n\nBy expanding the parentheses, we can write the equation in standard form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-x(2-\\sqrt{3}\\text{i})-x(2+\\sqrt{3}\\text{i})+(2+\\sqrt{3}\\text{i})(2-\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x+\\sqrt{3}x\\text{i}-2x-\\sqrt{3}x\\text{i}+4-(\\sqrt{3}\\text{i})^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-4x+4+3 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-4x+7 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation has roots $x=2\\pm \\sqrt{3}\\text{i},$ but the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ is not $2.$ Therefore, to find the desired equation, we multiply the equation above by $2.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2({x}^{2}-4x+7) & =0 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-8x+14 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308996 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0db14c2a-b6c3-5550-8ff6-c1a9e2b4ea5d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Quadratic Equation Given an Imaginary Root and Its Leading Coefficient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6a801935-d0fa-585b-ac1c-fdb44d39443e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10x^2+40" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308998}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6a801935-d0fa-585b-ac1c-fdb44d39443e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10x^2+40" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308998} :answer-field/id #uuid "48b8c144-ca6d-5fa6-92b8-3831c408f8c7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308997}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f1b34ac4-4b12-5a14-be90-8e87dc13e93f" :question/math-academy-id "q-274994" :question/problem "A quadratic equation in the variable $x$ with real coefficients has a root $x=-2\\text{i}.$ Given that the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ in the equation is $10,$ find the equation. Write your answer in the form $a{x}^{2}+bx+c=0,$ where $a,$ $b,$ and $c$ are real constants.\n\n{{field-1}}$=0$" :question/worked-solution "Since the quadratic equation has real coefficients and $x=-2\\text{i}$ is a root, its conjugate $x=2\\text{i}$ must also be a root. Therefore, using the zero product property, we can write a quadratic equation with the required roots as follows:\n\n$$\n(x-2\\text{i})(x+2\\text{i})=0\n$$\n\nMultiplying out the parentheses and simplifying gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-(2\\text{i})^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-4(-1) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+4 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation has roots $x=\\pm 2\\text{i},$ but the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ is not $10.$ Therefore, to find the desired equation, we multiply the equation above by $10.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10({x}^{2}+4) & =0 \\\\ 10{x}^{2}+40 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308999 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0236acfd-59d1-5bc8-9e60-1c76ddc536f5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Quadratic Equation Given a Complex Root and Its Leading Coefficient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8356454d-b49f-5d62-915d-46613aad928f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{x}^{2}-8x+5=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309001} {:answer/id #uuid "7ded88d4-ed79-54df-8d41-c83aaad2920c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{x}^{2}+8x-10=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309002} {:answer/id #uuid "490f7140-c7b2-5e77-a27c-258005a9a15a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{x}^{2}-8x+10=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309003} {:answer/id #uuid "3c828eb2-2017-5275-a9f7-30d008823640" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{x}^{2}-2x+10=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309004} {:answer/id #uuid "06ca2dcd-2198-5012-838d-5ff7c23936a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{x}^{2}-4x+5=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309005}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "490f7140-c7b2-5e77-a27c-258005a9a15a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{x}^{2}-8x+10=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309003} :answer-field/id #uuid "38a8962b-84c2-562f-8c12-f4d5e6437e3f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309000}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2a38a23c-d1f3-5a91-bfc6-a693e8120f55" :question/math-academy-id "q-28219" :question/problem "A quadratic equation in the variable $x$ with real coefficients has a root $x=2-\\text{i}.$ Given that the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ in the equation is $2$, find the equation." :question/worked-solution "Since the quadratic equation has real coefficients and $x=2-\\text{i}$ is a root, its conjugate $x=2+\\text{i}$ must also be a root. Therefore, using the zero product property, we can write a quadratic equation with the required roots as follows:\n\n$$\n(x-(2-\\text{i}))(x-(2+\\text{i}))=0\n$$\n\nBy expanding the parentheses, we can write the equation in standard form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-x(2+\\text{i})-x(2-\\text{i})+(2-\\text{i})(2+\\text{i}) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x-x\\text{i}-2x+x\\text{i}+4-{\\text{i}}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-4x+4+1 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-4x+5 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation has roots $x=2\\pm \\text{i},$ but the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ is not $2.$ Therefore, to find the desired equation, we multiply the equation above by $2.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2({x}^{2}-4x+5) & =0 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-8x+10 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186309006 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0236acfd-59d1-5bc8-9e60-1c76ddc536f5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Quadratic Equation Given a Complex Root and Its Leading Coefficient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "88c14fe2-5d37-5e2a-b95b-dfed2e23b7b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+9x+79=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309008} {:answer/id #uuid "84926b86-bc9d-5f77-ba7f-54bb9eddecab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+18x+79=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309009} {:answer/id #uuid "45926271-68d5-5a0c-962f-6440c0c84e92" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}-9x+79=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309010} {:answer/id #uuid "9ed25718-8e70-532b-ba6e-ef7fb0cd3e77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}-18x+83=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309011} {:answer/id #uuid "7246b455-cbb5-54e9-88ef-e6698691e7a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+18x+83=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309012}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7246b455-cbb5-54e9-88ef-e6698691e7a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+18x+83=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309012} :answer-field/id #uuid "8f787076-bcf7-523b-8a8a-e7286fed9fd5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309007}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f7d949ff-8cfc-5143-939a-c731eeef437a" :question/math-academy-id "q-8188" :question/problem "A quadratic equation in the variable $x$ with real coefficients has a root $x=-9+\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}.$ Given that the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ in the equation is $1$, find the equation." :question/worked-solution "Since the quadratic equation has real coefficients and $x=-9+\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}$ is a root, its conjugate $x=-9-\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}$ must also be a root. Therefore, using the zero product property, we can write a quadratic equation with the required roots as follows:\n\n$$\n(x-(-9+\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}))(x-(-9-\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}))=0\n$$\n\nBy expanding the parentheses, we can write the equation in standard form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-x(-9+\\sqrt{2}\\text{i})-x(-9-\\sqrt{2}\\text{i})+(-9+\\sqrt{2}\\text{i})(-9-\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+9x-x\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}+9x+x\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}+81-2{\\text{i}}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+18x+81+2 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+18x+83 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
